   "blocks": [
    {
     "original": {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "I offer a concise, intentionally disquieting argument about why any putative scientific Theory of Everything (**TOE**) is epistemically constrained. The claim is simple and paradoxical: a TOE formulated within the conceptual and methodological bounds of human science can only be a theory of what our instruments, languages, and logics permit it to be. True totality — an account that includes itself and that which transcends its axioms — requires forms of self-description and meta-ontological operations that lie outside the epistemic reach of contemporary science. The result is that a TOE is, in practice, a \"theory of most things,\" not of everything. I conclude with a dark irony: the most honest TOE may be the problem itself.",
      "level": null,
      "number": null,
      "name": "I offer a concise, intentionally disquieting argument about why any putative scientific Theory of Everything (**TOE**) is epistemically constrained. The claim is simple and paradoxical: a TOE formulated within the conceptual and methodological bounds of human science can only be a theory of what our instruments, languages, and logics permit it to be. True totality — an account that includes itself and that which transcends its axioms — requires forms of self-description and meta-ontological operations that lie outside the epistemic reach of contemporary science. The result is that a TOE is, in practice, a \"theory of most things,\" not of everything. I conclude with a dark irony: the most honest TOE may be the problem itself."
     },
     "everyone": {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Here's the argument in plain words. A Theory of Everything is supposed to explain — well, everything. But science builds its theories with human tools: our instruments, our languages, our logic. So the best a scientific TOE can ever manage is to explain everything *as those tools can see it*. The real totality — all of reality, including the very act of explaining — needs something science can't reach: a way for the theory to describe itself, and to talk about what lies beyond its own rules. So in practice, any scientific TOE ends up a \"theory of most things.\" Not everything. And here's the sting: maybe the most honest theory of everything is the problem itself — the impossibility of doing it at all.",
      "level": null,
      "number": null,
      "name": "Here's the argument in plain words. A Theory of Everything is supposed to explain — well, everything. But science builds its theories with human tools: our instruments, our languages, our logic. So the best a scientific TOE can ever manage is to explain everything *as those tools can see it*. The real totality — all of reality, including the very act of explaining — needs something science can't reach: a way for the theory to describe itself, and to talk about what lies beyond its own rules. So in practice, any scientific TOE ends up a \"theory of most things.\" Not everything. And here's the sting: maybe the most honest theory of everything is the problem itself — the impossibility of doing it at all."
     }
    }
   ],
